{:answer/id #uuid "e79b52d0-6b85-5411-84b7-5e56627db8e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186667790} :answer-field/id #uuid "b7efb14b-9f7d-5dfb-a0ce-9c62921da47e" :answer-field/key "None of the above" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186668172}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a2c02942-de68-547a-8bfa-7932a2f390a1" :question/math-academy-id "q-103438" :question/problem "![](images/5b/5bbb0cacbe8dca2facfd49e757ba9b7aa40ee91ae820f001c72d637186d57b79.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true with respect to the function $y=f(x)$ plotted above?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let's check our statements.\n\n- Statement “$f(x)$ has a critical point at $x=-1$” is true. The function has a critical point (a corner) at $x=-1.$ In this case,  ${f}^{′}(-1)$ does not exist.\n\n- Statement “$f(x)$ has a stationary point at $x=0$” is true. Stationary points are points where ${f}^{′}(x)=0.$ In this case, the graph of the function has a horizontal tangent line at $x=0,$ and therefore, ${f}^{′}(0)=0.$\n\n- Statement “$f(x)$ has a local minimum at $x=1$” is true. The function has a local minimum at $x=1$ since $f(1)\\le f(x)$ for all $x$ close enough to $x=1.$\n\nTherefore, all three statements are true." :task-item/_content [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186729512 :task-item/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186238006} :task-item/elapsed-seconds 28.62 :task-item/id #uuid "150a4d42-836a-4fb1-b42b-b6e9964659ec" :task-item/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186729513} :task-item/responses [{:answer/id #uuid "d2a8359a-378f-5d30-b9c0-f8c4d4fb9c4d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461280} {:answer/id #uuid "7d3f2ac5-9e21-5c73-81bb-3e709f16ae1a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461282} {:answer/id #uuid "9d12003a-0d3f-510e-b2aa-cfbebcab6a56" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461284} {:answer/id #uuid "e79b52d0-6b85-5411-84b7-5e56627db8e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186667790}] :task-item/status {:db/ident :task-item.status/correct} :task-item/working-ended-at #inst "2026-10-28T16:44:41.374Z"}]}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186238008 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064162 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a595bc07-ad64-537d-be42-06144814b97d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Extrema of a Function Given Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00b0d9d1-e47c-54ba-9b31-b33369664b09" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235800} {:answer/id #uuid "58c8a29d-6f8d-5816-90cd-1987524d814c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236376} {:answer/id #uuid "87aa58ba-7fe0-58ce-9c4e-cf7b09d0af52" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of those listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236664} {:answer/id #uuid "956d5ea6-81ef-5247-b6ca-c5c4ad5b1297" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236765} {:answer/id #uuid "a8f3abd4-2dce-5449-bff6-569d90a5f30d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236879}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "00b0d9d1-e47c-54ba-9b31-b33369664b09" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235800} :answer-field/id #uuid "d2db27c7-6dec-54c4-b324-46dcaa54ec46" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237713}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "941a791b-cbcb-5dd5-898e-ada869b3a20f" :question/math-academy-id "q-103442" :question/problem "![](images/7d/7dc7480c7ee27073ff7be7475161742e317b30bf0c3e35af33dc93a772e6034a.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true with respect to the function $y=f(x)$ plotted above?\n\n- $f(x)$ has a critical point at $x=-1$\n- $f(x)$ has a stationary point at $x=1$\n- $f(x)$ has a local maximum at $x=3$" :question/worked-solution "Let's check our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. The function has a critical point (a corner) at $x=-1.$ In this case,  ${f}^{′}(-1)$ does not exist.\n\n- Statement II is false. The function has a critical point (a corner) at $x=1.$ In this case,  ${f}^{′}(-1)$ does not exist.\n\n- Statement III is false. The function has a local minimum at $x=3$ since $f(3)\\le f(x)$ for all $x$ close enough to $x=3.$\n\nTherefore, statement I is true, while statements II and III are false."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329838 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064273 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6b5ea4a7-acc4-5825-b6c5-b6c87e6a1674" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Critical and Stationary Points of a Function Given Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "87183a17-7cd1-5a25-9904-a298705d4eaa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=2$ and $x=6$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328095} {:answer/id #uuid "90a93ac5-89f6-55a4-bfbf-35089b23e169" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=4$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328196} {:answer/id #uuid "a0749e1a-ee3d-512b-859b-7f4078d0db89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=2$ and $x=4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328322} {:answer/id #uuid "b771c0e3-9f5d-5460-91dc-d2a40dcc848e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=1$ and $x=4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328530} {:answer/id #uuid "d5fbcbfa-ac4d-5b6e-8484-60a678be421a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=3$ and $x=5$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328808}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a0749e1a-ee3d-512b-859b-7f4078d0db89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=2$ and $x=4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328322} :answer-field/id #uuid "87177835-8a97-5da6-8f56-68962f28430c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329407}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a815684f-b87f-5e55-b82d-ad4638cad69f" :question/math-academy-id "q-47911" :question/problem "![](images/bb/bb8959927fc561f9fc6e021b690c43f4ab57b169aa1526390e02c8d48386bfe3.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is plotted above. What are the $x$-coordinates of the critical points?" :question/worked-solution "The critical points of $f(x)$ are the values of $x$ where ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(x)$ doesn't exist. Graphically, those are the values where the tangent to the graph is horizontal or where the tangent does not exist, respectively. \n\nIn the given graph, we can see that \n\n- at $x=2$ the tangent to the graph is horizontal, so we have ${f}^{′}(0)=0,$ while\n\n- at $x=4$ there is a sharp corner, so ${f}^{′}(4)$ doesn't exist\n\n![](images/60/60f26a8aa758ae20b2b56325d822379d407276ffd6987f56a1e7934a6406c4d2.png)\n\n \n\nTherefore, there are two critical points, and their $x$-coordinates are $x=2$ and $x=4.$" :task-item/_content [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186729498 :task-item/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329838} :task-item/elapsed-seconds 13.139000000000001 :task-item/id #uuid "f961504c-45e8-49f8-9d42-6eabc15cc3bd" :task-item/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186729510} :task-item/responses [{:answer/id #uuid "a0749e1a-ee3d-512b-859b-7f4078d0db89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=2$ and $x=4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328322}] :task-item/status {:db/ident :task-item.status/correct} :task-item/working-ended-at #inst "2026-10-28T16:43:40.321Z"}]}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341964 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064273 :knowledge-point/i