en graph, we can see that \n\n- at $x=3$ the tangent to the graph is horizontal, so we have ${f}^{′}(3)=0,$ while\n\n- at $x=7$ there is a discontinuity, so  ${f}^{′}(7)$ doesn't exist.\n\n![](images/e0/e035d64e759108be78c54d0d8d1018906ef253bdcdaff548edd185b8b6543505.png)\n\n \n\nTherefore, there are two critical points, and their $x$-coordinates are $x=3$ and $x=7.$" :task-item/_content [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186729440 :task-item/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341964} :task-item/elapsed-seconds 37.998999999999995 :task-item/id #uuid "c9e913df-6481-4284-bf15-6fc79eee5170" :task-item/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186729497} :task-item/responses [{:answer/id #uuid "9bf1e416-8b63-5226-ad41-f7ddb3b203bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=3$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340531}] :task-item/status {:db/ident :task-item.status/incorrect} :task-item/working-ended-at #inst "2026-10-28T16:41:41.172Z"}]}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341965 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064273 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6b5ea4a7-acc4-5825-b6c5-b6c87e6a1674" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Critical and Stationary Points of a Function Given Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0aee028a-5473-56b8-aee5-bdb011708151" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=0$ and $x=2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339222} {:answer/id #uuid "38edbb45-8a8f-5c9f-89e6-a14df9e79c49" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=6$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339640} {:answer/id #uuid "4c17b524-97bf-5d55-bc46-acf8d26004be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=2$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339813} {:answer/id #uuid "62113950-85db-5a6d-98d6-ae248b3847ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=0$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340002} {:answer/id #uuid "d6ac01be-6526-5f9f-b3ca-56e460493d3b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=2$ and $x=6$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341031}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "62113950-85db-5a6d-98d6-ae248b3847ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=0$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340002} :answer-field/id #uuid "a711b931-a46e-513a-9ac0-e43329d1aa18" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341671}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3747ead0-6028-5b33-a0a9-4b842fbf83b2" :question/math-academy-id "q-73401" :question/problem "![](images/48/48569328379c72215884b759687c7c40ced06f9864d05d2638e4389694f90630.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is plotted above. What are the $x$-coordinates of the stationary points?" :question/worked-solution "A stationary point of $f(x)$ is the value of $x$ where ${f}^{′}(x)=0.$ Graphically, those are the values where the tangent to the graph is horizontal.\n\nIn the given graph, we can see that at $x=0,$ the tangent to the graph is horizontal, so we have ${f}^{′}(0)=0.$\n\n![](images/57/576d25a420f1f4ebcbd0e9b055d7a27159caa8f1721ff5f35a32b86c3e3a0fb3.png)\n\n  \n\nTherefore, there is only one stationary point, and its $x$-coordinate is $x=0.$\n\n**Note:** At $x=2$, there is a sharp corner, so ${f}^{′}(2)$ doesn't exist. This gives one more critical point. But this is not a stationary point." :task-item/_content [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186729422 :task-item/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341965} :task-item/elapsed-seconds 21.574 :task-item/id #uuid "069e03e8-6fd5-4015-81a8-2af125dcd7df" :task-item/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186729440} :task-item/responses [{:answer/id #uuid "62113950-85db-5a6d-98d6-ae248b3847ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=0$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340002}] :task-item/status {:db/ident :task-item.status/correct} :task-item/working-ended-at #inst "2026-10-28T16:40:26.086Z"}]}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186600826 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064273 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6b5ea4a7-acc4-5825-b6c5-b6c87e6a1674" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Critical and Stationary Points of a Function Given Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2888f6ea-cc59-59d8-a5bb-544b41c8c594" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=2$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186600538} {:answer/id #uuid "9189d99e-b17a-561b-bd3e-ce72cb3f6f90" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=8$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186600619} {:answer/id #uuid "b9cb8aeb-f507-5bdc-90aa-f92c9a456a0d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-1$, $x=2$,  and $x=8$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186600647} {:answer/id #uuid "d2686125-3d15-500a-915c-837b4d1dfc2e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=2$ and $x=8$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186600665} {:answer/id #uuid "d4e008e5-5a99-587a-84b0-5e37eb6c32b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-1$ and $x=5$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186600668}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d2686125-3d15-500a-915c-837b4d1dfc2e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=2$ and $x=8$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186600665} :answer-field/id #uuid "71d9e6ce-dbf0-5c02-a752-26f68cd829d6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186600741}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c7f1e70d-5c89-56c5-b289-958d7b590241" :question/math-academy-id "q-73400" :question/problem "![](images/99/991ced811f1c8f1e337025b6e2f7273d2b028a3af4a376efb8436956dc40fa62.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is plotted above. What are the $x$-coordinates of the critical points?" :question/worked-solution "The critical points of $f(x)$ are the values of $x$ where ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(x)$ doesn't exist. Graphically, those are the values where the tangent to the graph is horizontal or where the tangent does not exist, respectively. \n\nIn the given graph, we can see that \n\n- at $x=2$ there is a sharp corner, so ${f}^{′}(2)$ doesn't exist, and\n\n- at $x=8$ there is a discontinuity, so  ${f}^{′}(8)$ doesn't exist  either.\n\n![](images/cf/cf7b011f1cf09fecbdc60271ee17869b0260f7b2d3b03e26d8a675e38d1f2f7c.png)\n\n \n\nTherefore, there are two critical points, and their $x$-coordinates are $x=2$ and $x=8.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186600837 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064162 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a595bc07-ad64-537d-be42-06144814b97d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Extrema of a Function Given Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "44bf7efd-76e0-53c1-939a-1fe7df2df08a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186600558} {:answer/id #uuid "70f39c71-b0a3-59ac-b6d1-f7e833cef523" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186600591} {:answer/id #uuid "79b20085-7a3f-58fa-b8e0-90c289798593" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186600600} {:answer/id #uuid "e1f075db-4173-570a-9bf9-e5701275eaf0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186600681} {:answer/id #uuid "ee83d8ec-0c55-5717-bc71-16614770669e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186600691}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "70f39c71-b0a3-59ac-b6d1-f7e833cef523" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186600591} :answer-field/id #uuid "f66c229e-7713-50f3-babe-189f09d2d7f9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186600778}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fc518e9d-35b1-5473-8374-94a56f67a024" :question/math-academy-id "q-48736" :question/problem "![](images/36/36477ec421ff4b7f349a060df762eadee83b008fbfc9e021a8893cc45e405cae.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true with respect to the function $y=f(x)$ plotted above?\n\nI. $f(x)$ has  a stationary point at $x=2$\n\nII. $f(x)$ has  a local maximum at $x=-2$\n\nIII. $f(x)$ has a stationary point at $x=-2$" :question/worked-solution "Let's check our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Stationary points are points where ${f}^{′}(x)=0.$ In this case,  the graph of the function has a horizontal tangent line at $x=2$ and therefore, ${f}^{′}(2)=0.$\n\n- Statement II is true. The function has a local maximum at $x=-2$ since $f(-2)\\ge f(x)$ for all $x$ close enough  to $x=-2.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Stationary points are points where ${f}^{′}(x)=0.$ In this case,  the graph of the function has a sharp edge at $x=-2$ and therefore, ${f}^{′}(-2)$ does not exist.\n\nTherefore, only statements I and II are true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186620891 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064162 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a595bc07-ad64-537d-be42-06144814b97d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Extrema of a Function Given Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16815d31-7411-5233-9cb1-0a0dd68d34fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186620000} {:answer/id #uuid "3509f24d-1a31-5d2f-8bc6-6055d61503eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186620097} {:answer/id #uuid "512bb666-b885-5f43-bbf5-76508401fdf0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186620183} {:answer/id #uuid "c4be2ac3-fe36-5cb3-8474-cda6904a0621" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186620498} {:answer/id #uuid "c4ff53c7-127d-5ab7-8922-654475e9faa1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186620501}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3509f24d-1a31-5d2f-8bc6-6055d61503eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186620097} :answer-field/id #uuid "fa08c47a-165b-5e75-ba82-50c7e3bfa2ae" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186620807}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f9fa6c95-e7a8-572d-883e-45f18ba990a5" :question/math-academy-id "q-47693" :question/problem "![](images/da/daba5c9c96666b81c5e0e3c0623aa97e234b208dc90f9a3f57785bfcc17552d9.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is plotted above. Given that $f(x)$ is defined on the interval $x\\in [-10,2]$, which points represent the global minimum of $f(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "The global extrema of a function are the lowest or highest points of the function's graph over its entire domain.\n\nThe lowest point is the global minimum, whereas the highest point is the global maximum.\n\nAccording to the graph, the lowest point is $A,$ and so this point represents the global minimum of $f(x).$ \n\n**Note:** The point $C$ is not a global minimum, it's a local minimum."}]}
